{#
   One screenshot, two widths.

   The exports are 420×900 and 900×1928, so that is what `intrinsic_height`
   defaults to. It is stated on the tag so the browser can reserve space before
   the image arrives (CLS). Note landing.css carries `img[width][height] {
   height: auto }` — without it those attributes fight the `width: 100%` rules
   and squash the image to a literal pixel height.

   `intrinsic_height` is a parameter because not every shot is a phone capture:
   matchday-ticker-live is a 420×174 banner. The attributes only set the
   pre-load aspect box (height:auto takes over once the real image lands), so a
   wrong value is invisible after load and a ~340px jump before it — which is
   exactly the layout shift these attributes exist to prevent. Pass the real
   height for anything that isn't a 420×900 phone capture.

   `sizes` must describe the CSS width the image will actually occupy, or the
   browser picks from srcset against the wrong number: the gallery frames are
   190px, and a default of 420px made every one of them download the 900w file.

   `eager` is for the first hero slide only — everything else is below the fold.
#}
{% macro img(name, alt, eager = false, display_width = 420, folder = '2026-08', intrinsic_height = 900) %}
    {%- set base = '/screenshots/' ~ folder ~ '/' ~ name -%}
    {%- set src = display_width > 460 ? base ~ '-900.webp' : base ~ '-420.webp' -%}
    <img src="{{ src }}"
         srcset="{{ base }}-420.webp 420w, {{ base }}-900.webp 900w"
         sizes="(max-width: 480px) 90vw, {{ display_width }}px"
         width="420" height="{{ intrinsic_height }}"
         alt="{{ alt }}"
         {% if eager %}loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"{% else %}loading="lazy" decoding="async"{% endif %}>
{% endmacro %}
